    // Optimized Halide pipeline: compute one *diagonal* of the DP table
    // per realization, instead of one scalar cell per realization.
    //
    // Original C kernel (PolyBench nussinov):
    //
    //   for (i = N-1; i >= 0; i--) {
    //     for (j = i+1; j < N; j++) {
    //       best = table[i][j];
    //       best = max(best, table[i][j-1]);
    //       best = max(best, table[i+1][j]);
    //       if (i < j-1)
    //         best = max(best, table[i+1][j-1] + match(seq[i], seq[j]));
    //       else
    //         best = max(best, table[i+1][j-1]);
    //       for (k = i+1; k < j; k++)
    //         best = max(best, table[i][k] + table[k+1][j]);
    //       table[i][j] = best;
    //     }
    //   }
    //
    // All dependencies for cell (i,j) are on entries with strictly
    // smaller distance d = j-i: (i,j-1), (i+1,j), (i+1,j-1),
    // (i,k), (k+1,j) all use d' < d. Therefore all cells on a
    // fixed diagonal d can be computed in parallel once all
    // smaller diagonals have been computed.
    //
    // We exploit this by:
    //   * Outer host loop over d = 1..N-1
    //   * For each d, a 1D Halide Func computes all i (0..N-d-1)
    //     on that diagonal in one realization.
    //   * The Func only reads from previously computed entries in
    //     'table' (smaller diagonals); we then write the new
    //     diagonal back to 'table' on the host.
